Scope
-----
This license applies to the Parlance *specification surface* — the files that
define the narrative data format and its runtime semantics, the reference tools
for checking conformance to them, and the editorial audits written against them:

  schema/                   the format definition (JSON Schema)
  tooling/conformance/      executable conformance vectors
  tooling/validate.py       the standalone reference validator
  tooling/requirements.txt  that validator's dependencies
  tooling/RUNTIME_CONTRACT.md
  tooling/INTEGRATION.md
  tooling/NAMING_STANDARDS.md
  tooling/VERSIONING.md
  tooling/MIGRATIONS.md
  tooling/audits/          review-only editorial audits (Claude skills)
  tooling/importers/       format importers, Yarn / Ink / Twine (Claude skills)
  tooling/PUBLISHED_SKILLS.md  the index to those two bundles

Copying these is the intent, not a leak. An engine port cannot be written
without them, and "engine-agnostic" is not a checkable claim unless anyone may
vendor the conformance vectors and run them. The audits are here on the same
test: they are review-only skills a narrative team is meant to run, fork and
adapt against their own project, which is not possible under a licence that
withholds the right to modify. They read a project and report; they never write
prose and never edit `data/`. The importers are here on the same reasoning:
conversion is not authorship, every string they emit came from the source file
byte for byte, and a project in an unusual dialect needs them forked.

ONE EXCEPTION, inside a path listed above. `tooling/importers/examples/` holds
worked migrations of third-party stories: the author's original source file and
the Parlance project a faithful import of it produces. That content is NOT ours
and this document does not grant anything over it. Each example carries its
upstream licence verbatim in its own LICENSE file, with the commit it was taken
from recorded in SOURCE.md, and it is redistributed under those terms and no
others. What IS granted here is everything else under `tooling/importers/` —
the skills, the parsers, the gate — and the prose we wrote about each example.

Everything else in this repository is licensed separately and is NOT covered by
this document — in particular the editor (`editor/`), which is governed by
LICENSE, and the game content in `data/`, `lore/`, and `tests/`, which is not
licensed for any use. Where the two documents could be read to overlap, this
one governs the paths listed above and LICENSE governs the rest.

